---
import ProfileLinks from "../components/ProfileLinks.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import PhotoStrip from "../components/PhotoStrip.astro";
import FieldPlate from "../components/FieldPlate.astro";
import ChainModel from "../components/ChainModel.astro";
import papers from "../data/papers.json";
import "../interior.css";

const journal = papers.filter((paper) => paper.type.startsWith("Journal"));
const preprints = papers
  .filter((paper) => paper.type.startsWith("Preprint"))
  .sort((first, second) => second.details.localeCompare(first.details));
const publishedAndPosted = [...journal, ...preprints];
const related = papers.filter(
  (paper) =>
    paper.type.startsWith("Working") || paper.type.startsWith("Public"),
);
---

<Layout
  title="Quantum physicist"
  description="Souvik Ghosh’s quantum computing experiments, independent theory, Vishwa Vigyan Labs projects, papers and academic CV."
>
  <article class="folio science" id="science">
    <header class="profile-opening">
      <div class="opening-copy">
        <span class="stamp">Souvik Ghosh / Research</span>
        <h1>Quantum physicist</h1>
        <p class="intro">
          At the <a href="#lab">Quantum Circuit Lab</a> in Taiwan, I cool
          superconducting circuits and use microwaves to measure them. On paper,
          I study a different question. Why do some quantum waves spread while
          others crowd against a boundary?
        </p>
        <div class="profile-actions">
          <a class="profile-button" href="/Souvik-Ghosh-CV.pdf" download>
            Download academic CV · PDF
          </a>
          <a href="#papers">Papers</a>
          <a href="#student">Start with a model ↓</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <FieldPlate
        photo="fridge-work"
        label="Experimental physics / Kaohsiung"
        caption="Working on the lower stages of the dilution refrigerator, where the circuit is cooled for measurement."
      />
    </header>

    <section class="folio-row" id="lab">
      <div>
        <span class="stamp">At the bench</span>
        <h2>What does a qubit tell us?</h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          My experimental work is at the Quantum Circuit Lab in the Department
          of Physics at National Sun Yat-sen University, Taiwan. My advisor is
          Assoc. Prof. Kuei-Lin Chiu.
        </p>
        <p>
          A qubit has a characteristic transition frequency. By sweeping a
          microwave tone and looking for a change in the readout, I can locate
          that transition. The next question is how long an excitation survives
          after the drive stops.
        </p>
        <PhotoStrip
          items={[
            ["under-plate", "Beneath the lowest plate"],
            ["circuit-detail", "A circuit assembly in hand"],
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="folio-row" id="student">
      <div>
        <span class="stamp">Try the physics</span>
        <h2>Can a wave lean against a wall?</h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          Imagine 17 sites in a row. A particle can hop to either neighbour. The
          bars below show how one wave is distributed along the chain. Give the
          two directions different hopping strengths and see where the weight
          goes.
        </p>
        <ChainModel />
        <p>
          This is a textbook model with open ends. My{" "}
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.20330">sole-authored preprint</a>{" "}
          asks what changes when the chain has alternating bonds and a second
          pattern that tends to pin the wave in place. In part of the phase
          diagram, an intermediate modulation lets states spread out again.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="https://vishwavigyan.com/non-hermitian.html">
            Explore the larger model at Vishwa Vigyan Labs →
          </a>
        </p>
        <p>
          If you’re studying this for the first time, try explaining what
          changed when you reversed the bias.{" "}
          <a href="mailto:souvikghosh2012@gmail.com?subject=Question%20about%20the%20quantum%20chain">
            Write to me about the part that surprised you.
          </a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="folio-row" id="theory">
      <div>
        <span class="stamp">Independent theory</span>
        <h2>When training looks stable</h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          The mathematics behind directed hopping has a second life in machine
          learning. A small disturbance can grow for several steps even when the
          usual long-term stability check looks reassuring.
        </p>
        <p>
          In{" "}
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.23476">
            my preprint on neural-network training
          </a>
          , I use small two-layer networks to test a quantity that tracks this
          temporary growth. It separated stable and unstable cases in those
          experiments.
        </p>
        <p>
          My other line of theory looks at electron transport through tiny
          conductors, including what measurements can tell us about where charge
          accumulates.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="folio-row">
      <h2>Vishwa Vigyan Labs</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          My independent work has its own home at Vishwa Vigyan. The project
          pages include models you can try and distinguish published work from
          open questions.
        </p>
        <ul class="project-list">
          <li>
            <a href="https://vishwavigyan.com/non-hermitian.html">
              Non-Hermitian systems
            </a>{" "}
            — why a wave can gather at the edge of a chain.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://vishwavigyan.com/quantum-time-lab.html">
              Quantum Time Lab
            </a>{" "}
            — an ongoing study of transport and the interpretation of time.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://vishwavigyan.com/bigbang-atlas.html">
              Big Bang Atlas
            </a>{" "}
            — an early exploration of cosmology with Prof. Prosenjit Singha Deo.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://vishwavigyan.com/quantum-ai.html">Quantum AI</a> —
            experiments and an interactive illustration of a driven qubit.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <a href="/inventor/">
          For telescope and engineering work, visit my inventor profile →
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="folio-row" id="papers">
      <h2>Papers</h2>
      <div>
        <ol class="bibliography">
          {publishedAndPosted.map((paper) => (
            <li>
              <span class="folio-meta">{paper.type}</span>
              <h3>
                <a href={paper.url!}>{paper.title}</a>
              </h3>
              <p>{paper.authors}</p>
              <p class="record">{paper.details}</p>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
        <details>
          <summary>Related writing and work in progress</summary>
          <ul class="bibliography">
            {related.map((item) => (
              <li>
                <span class="folio-meta">{item.type}</span>
                <h3>
                  {item.url ? <a href={item.url}>{item.title}</a> : item.title}
                </h3>
                <p>{item.authors}</p>
                <p class="record">{item.details}</p>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="folio-row">
      <h2>Research background</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          I completed my M.Sc. in Physics at Adamas University in 2024,
          specializing in condensed matter. My dissertation studied how a
          quasiperiodic layer changes reflection at a junction between a metal
          and a superconductor.
        </p>
        <p>
          My earlier research at the S. N. Bose National Centre for Basic
          Sciences in Kolkata focused on mesoscopic transport.
        </p>
        <p>
          For research enquiries, write to{" "}
          <a href="mailto:souvikghosh2012@gmail.com">
            souvikghosh2012@gmail.com
          </a>
          .
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <ProfileLinks related={["inventor", "wellness-rd"]} />
  </article>
</Layout>
<style>
  .bibliography {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .bibliography li {
    padding-bottom: 1.2rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 25%, transparent);
  }
  .bibliography h3 {
    font-size: clamp(1.18rem, 2.5vw, 1.55rem);
  }
  .bibliography p {
    margin: 0.15em 0;
  }
  .bibliography .record {
    font-size: 0.88em;
  }
  summary {
    cursor: pointer;
    padding: 0.5rem 0;
  }
  details[open] .bibliography {
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
</style>
